    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The Indian Penal Code (IPC), 1860 serves as the primary substantive criminal law defining offences and penalties.",
        "The Code of Criminal Procedure (CrPC), 1973 details procedural rules for arrest, investigation, bail, and court trials.",
        "The Indian Evidence Act, 1872 determines the admissibility and relevancy of evidence presented during judicial proceedings.",
        "Cognizable offences grant police authority to arrest without a judicial warrant under Section 2(c) of CrPC.",
        "First Information Report (FIR) is recorded under Section 154 of CrPC for cognizable offences."
      ],
      "id": "81oyj3b"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Distinction between Cognizable (Section 2(c)) and Non-Cognizable (Section 2(l)) offences is frequently tested in WB Police exams.",
        "Remember key sections: FIR (Section 154 CrPC), Arrest without warrant (Section 41 CrPC), and Anticipatory Bail (Section 438 CrPC).",
        "Questions often differentiate Culpable Homicide (Section 299 IPC) from Murder (Section 300 IPC)."
      ],
      "id": "qqy33k1"
    },
